.TH HTTPCLIENT 2
.SH NAME
httpclient: Hdr, Resp, init, get, post, request \- HTTP/1.1 client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "httpclient.m";
http := load Httpclient Httpclient->PATH;

Hdr: adt {
	name:	string;
	val:	string;
};

Resp: adt {
	code:	int;
	reason:	string;
	hdrs:	list of Hdr;
	body:	array of byte;

	hdr:	fn(r: self ref Resp, name: string): string;
};

init:		fn(): string;
get:		fn(url: string, hdrs: list of Hdr): (ref Resp, string);
post:		fn(url: string, hdrs: list of Hdr, body: array of byte): (ref Resp, string);
request:	fn(method: string, url: string, hdrs: list of Hdr,
			body: array of byte): (ref Resp, string);
requestat:	fn(method: string, url: string, addr: string,
			hdrs: list of Hdr, body: array of byte): (ref Resp, string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Httpclient
makes an HTTP/1.1 request and returns the reply.
.PP
.B Init
must be called first and returns nil, or a diagnostic if a module it
needs will not load.
.PP
.BR Get ,
.B post
and
.B request
each return a
.B Resp
and a nil error string, or nil and a diagnostic.  A reply that is not
200 is not an error: the status is in
.BR Resp.code ,
and it is for the caller to decide.
.PP
.B Resp.hdr
looks up a reply header by name, case-insensitively as the protocol
requires, and returns nil if it is absent.
.PP
Every request is sent with
.BR "Connection: close" .
That costs a connection per request and in exchange removes every
question about where one reply ends and the next begins.  Replies are
read with a
.BR Content-Length ,
as
.B chunked
transfer-encoding, or to end of file, in that order of preference.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/httpclient.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hget (1),
.IR cloudfs (4),
.IR url (2),
.IR dial (2)
.PP
An
.B https
URL goes through
.IR tls12 (2),
which is loaded on first use so that a caller who only ever reads a
metadata service does not pay for the crypto.  The server's chain is
verified against
.B /lib/tls/ca-bundle.pem
and must name the host; either failure aborts the request rather than
returning a reply.
.PP
.B Requestat
dials
.I addr
instead of resolving the URL's host, while still sending that host as
.BR Host: ,
naming it in SNI and requiring the certificate to match \(em it
connects to one place and authenticates another, which
.IR curl (1)
spells
.BR --connect-to .
It is there because
.B /net/cs
resolves no names under hosted
.IR emu (1),
so without it none of this could be tested against a real server.
.SH BUGS
There is no redirect following, no authentication, and no connection
reuse.
.PP
TLS 1.2 only, and the key exchange is X25519 only.
